An import brings a whole Scrivener project across: its binder, its text, its synopses and
notes, its comments and footnotes, its labels and its status list. It creates a **new**
Skribisto project. Your Scrivener project is only read, never changed, so you can go back to
it at any time.

For a project made in Manuskript or Plume Creator, see
[bringing a whole project across](:help-import-projects).

## Starting an import

With a project open, choose *Work ▸ Import from ▸ Scrivener (.scriv)…*. In the Launcher, the
same import sits behind *Create from…*. You can also type *Import Scrivener Project…* in the
command palette (*Help ▸ Command Palette…*).

The import has three pages: *Project*, *Review* and *Save*. Nothing is written until you
press *Import* on the last one.

## Choosing the project

A Scrivener project looks different depending on where you work:

- **On Windows and Linux** a project is a folder whose name ends in `.scriv`. Choose it with
  *Choose a folder…*, or open it and choose the `.scrivx` file inside with *Choose a file…*.
- **On a Mac** a project shows as a single file when Scrivener is installed. Choose it with
  *Choose a file…*. If it shows as a folder, use *Choose a folder…*.

You can also drop the project on the *Project* page, or type or paste its path.

A backup made by Scrivener works as it is, without unpacking it: choose the `.zip`. A
`.scrivtemplate` file works too, and makes a new project from the template.

Projects from Scrivener 3 and Scrivener 2 on the Mac, from Scrivener on Windows, from the
Scrivener for Linux beta, and projects made on an iPhone or iPad can all be imported. A
project from Scrivener 1 on the Mac must first be opened once in Scrivener 2 or 3.

## What the import checks first

As soon as you choose a project, the *Project* page says which Scrivener last saved it,
when, and how many documents it holds. It also tells you about anything that needs your
attention:

- **Several binder files.** Scrivener keeps the list of your documents in a `.scrivx` file,
  and a sync service sometimes leaves extra copies of it. Choose the one you last worked in.
  When the page can tell which one that is, it is already selected.
- **The copy kept before an update.** When Scrivener moves a project to a newer version, it
  keeps the old one beside it, with "Backup" added to its name. The page offers the project
  you went on writing in.
- **Changes from an iPhone or iPad.** If Scrivener on an iPhone or iPad changed the project
  since it was last opened on a computer, those changes are not in the files yet. Open the
  project once in Scrivener on your computer first, or tick *Import it without those
  changes*.
- **A copy with none of its text.** If a sync service has not finished copying the project,
  its documents would arrive empty. Wait for the copy to finish, or tick *Import the
  documents empty*.
- **A project still open in Scrivener.** Close it there first, so that every change is
  saved.

Copies of your documents left behind by a sync service come across in a folder of their
own, so you can compare them with the documents. Extra copies of Scrivener's own settings
files are set aside, as Scrivener itself does.

## Reviewing before you import

*Review* shows the binder of the new project before any of it exists. *Becomes* says what
each item will be, and *In exports* whether it is included when you export the book.

Beside the tree, the choices change how the binder comes across:

- *Levels* says what the folders and documents at each level become, such as *Parts*,
  *Chapters* or *Scenes*.
- *Top-level folders* says where each folder beside your manuscript goes: *Notes*, *Story
  bible*, *Another book*, or *Leave out* for one you do not want to bring.
- *Bring the Trash across* brings the items in Scrivener's Trash, in Skribisto's Trash.
- *Blank lines become scene breaks* and *Lines of ornaments become scene breaks* decide what
  becomes a scene break in your text.

Under the tree, *Before you import* lists what the import does with your binder, and what
it leaves behind, so you know before you import.

Every item can be retyped afterwards from the binder. See
[the writing model](:help-writing-model) for what each type is.

## Saving the new project

*Save* asks for a *Destination folder* and a *File name*, beside your Scrivener project by
default. It also shows the *Title*, *Author* and *Language* your project states. Change any
of them before you import.

When your research holds files Skribisto cannot open, such as PDF files or sounds, *Copy
research files beside the new project* puts them in a folder next to it.

## What Scrivener brings with it

- Your text, in binder order, with its bold, italics, lists, tables and pictures.
- Each document's synopsis, as its synopsis in Skribisto.
- Each document's notes, as a note beside it.
- Your comments and footnotes, including the ones written inside the text. An inline
  annotation becomes a comment.
- Your labels as colored tags, and your status list as the project's statuses.
- The "Include in Compile" box, which becomes *Include in exports*.
- Your document templates, as note templates.
- The pictures in your research, each in a note of its own.

## What has no home here

- **Snapshots.** Your earlier versions stay in your Scrivener project.
- **Keywords, custom metadata, collections and binder icons.**
- **Text color, highlighting and fonts.** Skribisto sets the look of the text itself.
- **Research files that are not pictures**, such as PDF files, sound or web pages. Each one
  becomes a note that names the file, which stays in your Scrivener project unless you copy
  it beside the new one.
- **Placeholders** such as `<$wc>` or `<$title>`. They are kept as plain text: replace them
  by hand.
- **Compile settings and the record of words written each day.** Set up your export in
  Skribisto. See [exporting](:help-export).

## After the import

A message says how many items the new project holds and offers *Open now*, which opens the
new project in a window of its own. When the import has more to report, the message stays
until you close it.

*See report* opens a report of the import. It says what came across and what you chose,
then lists the rest in four parts:

- *Could not be read*: text, comments, pictures or files the import could not read, or
  that are missing from the copy.
- *Check these*: what you should look at in the new project.
- *Brought across differently*: what changed shape on the way, or was dropped because
  Skribisto has no place for it.
- *Left behind*: what stays in your Scrivener project.

Each line names the documents concerned, as they are called in your Scrivener binder. *Copy
report* copies the whole report, to keep it or send it.

Skribisto keeps the reports of your latest imports until it closes. To open one again,
press *See report* while the message is up, or choose *Show Scrivener Import Report* in the
command palette of a project's window. In the new project's window, each document the report
names opens with a click.

The new project is a single `.skrib` file. If you prefer a folder, use *Work ▸ Save as
folder…* afterwards.

## If the import cannot read your project

The *Project* page says why, and what to do. Most often it is enough to open the project
once in Scrivener, which repairs and updates it, and then to import it again.

If that is not possible, a Word document is the way across. In Scrivener, choose "File ▸
Compile…" and compile for Word. In the compile format, give chapter titles the "Heading 1"
style and section titles "Heading 2", and keep comments as margin comments. Then, in
Skribisto, choose *Work ▸ Import from ▸ Documents (Markdown, Word, ODT)…*, and on its
*Review* page set *Heading levels* so that the first level becomes a Chapter and the second
a Scene. See [importing documents](:help-import-documents).

Named styles such as "Heading 1" exist only in Scrivener 3. From an older Scrivener, export
one file per document instead, as Word (.docx) or OpenDocument (.odt) files, and import
them together. Do not keep Rich Text (.rtf), the format Scrivener offers first: Skribisto
does not import it.

A compiled document carries your text, with its pictures, your comments and your
footnotes, but not your synopses, notes, labels, snapshots or the files in your research
folder.

